      enemy_name: 'The Irrelevant Lord',
      enemy_type: 'Rhetoric Warden',
      pre_narrative: `"Two questions remain," the Irrelevant Lord says. The scroll-pile throne seems to deflate slightly. "These final two are drawn from a single passage. Read it carefully. It contains a relevance problem AND an organizational problem, and they are related. Also, I inserted both of them. I am rather proud." He presents a long passage.`,
      passage: `[1] The most consequential moment in Grammar Keep's intellectual history was not the founding but the First Schism, when the Prescriptivists and Descriptivists formally separated into distinct scholarly factions. [2] The Schism produced the keep's most vibrant period of scholarship — each faction, sharpened against the other, produced arguments of unprecedented rigor. [3] It also produced decades of institutional dysfunction, as the two factions competed for resources, positions, and the ear of the Grammar Council. [4] The First Schism occurred in the year 412 of the Keep's calendar. [5] What is remarkable about this period is that the institutional dysfunction and the intellectual vibrancy were not separate phenomena — the dysfunction was the engine of the vibrancy. [6] Without the competition, the friction, and the urgency of having a credible opponent, neither faction might have developed the sophistication of argument that the Keep's Fifth Age is remembered for.`,
      question_stem: `The writer is considering deleting sentence [4] from the paragraph. Should sentence [4] be deleted? (Question 1 of 2)`,
