      pre_narrative: `The Irrelevant Lord rises from his scroll-pile throne for the final time. "Twenty," he says. "The last question uses the same passage. An organization problem I planted carefully. If you solve it, you defeat me. If you fail—" He straightens slightly. "Then the Rhetoric Ruins remain mine. Choose carefully, Scholar."`,
      passage: `[1] The most consequential moment in Grammar Keep's intellectual history was not the founding but the First Schism, when the Prescriptivists and Descriptivists formally separated into distinct scholarly factions. [2] The Schism produced the keep's most vibrant period of scholarship — each faction, sharpened against the other, produced arguments of unprecedented rigor. [3] It also produced decades of institutional dysfunction, as the two factions competed for resources, positions, and the ear of the Grammar Council. [5] What is remarkable about this period is that the institutional dysfunction and the intellectual vibrancy were not separate phenomena — the dysfunction was the engine of the vibrancy. [6] Without the competition, the friction, and the urgency of having a credible opponent, neither faction might have developed the sophistication of argument that the Keep's Fifth Age is remembered for.`,
